```swift
// iOS: exposing device battery level to JavaScript (React Native)
@objc(BatteryModule)
class BatteryModule: NSObject {
  @objc
  func getBatteryLevel(_ resolve: @escaping RCTPromiseResolveBlock,
                        reject: @escaping RCTPromiseRejectBlock) {
    UIDevice.current.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled = true
    resolve(UIDevice.current.batteryLevel)
  }
}
```

```javascript
// JavaScript side
import { NativeModules } from 'react-native';
const level = await NativeModules.BatteryModule.getBatteryLevel();
```

The native side does the actual platform-specific work; the JS side gets a plain async function call. This exact shape — thin native wrapper, promise-based JS interface — is the standard pattern for any platform capability the framework doesn't cover, and the Flutter/Dart equivalent (`MethodChannel`) follows the identical structure with different syntax.
